      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the number of four-digit odd numbers, \\(A\\), and the number of four-digit multiples of 5, \\(B\\), and then sum these two values.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate \\(A\\)\nAn even number cannot be a four-digit number, and odd numbers must end in one of the digits \\(1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\). Therefore, the last digit of a four-digit odd number can be chosen in 5 ways.\n\nFor the first digit (thousands place), it can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices, since it cannot be 0).\nFor the second digit (hundreds place), it can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\nFor the third digit (tens place), it can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\nFor the fourth digit (units place), it can be any of \\(1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\) (5 choices).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit odd numbers is:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate \\(B\\)\nA number is a multiple of 5 if its last digit is either \\(0\\) or \\(5\\). Therefore, for the last digit, we have 2 choices (\\(0\\) or \\(5\\)).\nFor the first digit (thousands place), it can be any digit from 1 to 9 (9 choices, since it cannot be 0).\nFor the second digit (hundreds place), it can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\nFor the third digit (tens place), it can be any digit from 0 to 9 (10 choices).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 is:\n\\[\nB = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 2 = 1800\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate \\(A + B\\)\nNow, we sum the number of four-digit odd numbers and the number of four-digit multiples of 5:\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 1800 = 6300\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6300}\n\\]",
