      "text": "To find the number of four-digit odd numbers \\( A \\), we start by noting that a four-digit number ranges from 1000 to 9999. For a number to be odd, its last digit must be one of \\( \\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\\} \\). \n\nThe number of choices for the last digit is 5. The first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 for the first digit, and 0 to 9 for the second, third, and fourth digits. Therefore, each of the first three digits contributes 9 choices. The fourth digit has 10 choices (0 through 9).\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit odd numbers \\( A \\) is calculated as:\n\\[\nA = 9 \\times 10 \\times 10 \\times 5 = 4500\n\\]\n\nNext, we determine the number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\( B \\). For a number to be a multiple of 5, its last digit must be either 0 or 5. We will consider these cases separately.\n\n### Case 1: The last digit is 0\nIf the last digit is 0, the number is a four-digit number.\nThe first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 for each digit, giving \\( 9 \\times 9 \\times 9 = 729 \\) possibilities.\n\n### Case 2: The last digit is 5\nIf the last digit is 5, the number is a four-digit number.\nThe first three digits can be any digit from 1 to 9 for each digit, giving \\( 9 \\times 9 \\times 9 = 729 \\) possibilities.\n\nThus, the total number of four-digit multiples of 5 \\( B \\) is:\n\\[\nB = 729 + 729 = 1458\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find \\( A + B \\):\n\\[\nA + B = 4500 + 1458 = 5958\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{5958}\n\\]",
